1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,216
Welcome to chapter five of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,216 --> 00:00:12,464
This is where part two begins, and where we stop drawing diagrams and start writing code.

3
00:00:12,464 --> 00:00:26,433
In this chapter we'll run a real language model in a few lines, learn the Python we need to understand what just happened, and build our own tokenizer from scratch, the very first stage of every LLM.

4
00:00:26,433 --> 00:00:33,524
I ran the book's chapter five notebook end to end for this video, and I'll show you the real outputs.

5
00:00:34,374 --> 00:00:37,825
In part one we learned how LLMs work on paper.

6
00:00:37,825 --> 00:00:44,578
Part two gives us the tools: Python, NumPy, PyTorch, tokenizers and small neural networks.

7
00:00:44,578 --> 00:00:49,905
Today we run GPT-2, learn the Python essentials, and build a tokenizer.

8
00:00:49,905 --> 00:00:58,459
Everything follows the book's chapter five notebook, which you can open in Colab from the link in the description.

9
00:00:59,309 --> 00:01:00,612
Here's the roadmap.

10
00:01:00,612 --> 00:01:04,521
We start with the hook: running GPT-2 in about ten lines.

11
00:01:04,521 --> 00:01:05,618
Then why Python.

12
00:01:05,618 --> 00:01:09,664
Then the basics: variables, strings, numbers and f-strings.

13
00:01:09,664 --> 00:01:12,681
Lists and dictionaries give us a vocabulary.

14
00:01:12,681 --> 00:01:15,698
Control flow and loops handle unknown words.

15
00:01:15,698 --> 00:01:18,578
Functions give us tokenize and detokenize.

16
00:01:18,578 --> 00:01:23,653
A class packages it all into a SimpleTokenizer shaped like Hugging Face's.

17
00:01:23,653 --> 00:01:28,727
Then we come full circle, solve the exercises and do the review questions.

18
00:01:29,578 --> 00:01:31,419
The vocabulary for today.

19
00:01:31,419 --> 00:01:34,440
A variable is a name attached to a value.

20
00:01:34,440 --> 00:01:39,081
A string is text, which is what language models read and write.

21
00:01:39,081 --> 00:01:43,944
A list is an ordered collection, perfect for a sequence of tokens.

22
00:01:43,944 --> 00:01:49,249
A dictionary maps keys to values, which is exactly what a vocabulary is.

23
00:01:49,249 --> 00:01:53,227
A function is reusable code with inputs and an output.

24
00:01:53,227 --> 00:01:59,121
A class is a blueprint that bundles data and functions, like a tokenizer object.

25
00:01:59,121 --> 00:02:02,510
A tokenizer converts text to numbers and back.

26
00:02:02,510 --> 00:02:08,036
And a token is one unit of text, a word, a piece of a word, or a character.

27
00:02:08,885 --> 00:02:09,733
Part one.

28
00:02:09,733 --> 00:02:10,580
The hook.

29
00:02:10,580 --> 00:02:12,085
Let's run GPT-2.

30
00:02:12,936 --> 00:02:15,673
Here's the cell from the book's notebook.

31
00:02:15,673 --> 00:02:23,953
pipeline, from Hugging Face's transformers library, downloads and loads GPT-2, a hundred and twenty four million parameters.

32
00:02:23,953 --> 00:02:34,169
Then we call the generator with a prompt, the secret to building AI is, ask for twenty new tokens, and turn on sampling so the output is a little random.

33
00:02:34,169 --> 00:02:36,907
The pad token ID just silences a warning.

34
00:02:36,907 --> 00:02:37,574
That's it.

35
00:02:37,574 --> 00:02:41,714
You're driving one of the most important models in AI history.

36
00:02:42,564 --> 00:02:46,142
And here's what I got when I ran it on a plain CPU.

37
00:02:46,142 --> 00:02:51,264
The secret to building AI is to know that people are not afraid to learn.

38
00:02:51,264 --> 00:02:55,614
Then it wanders off into conducted by the National Academy of.

39
00:02:55,614 --> 00:03:01,087
Your output will be different, because sampling draws random tokens each time.

40
00:03:01,087 --> 00:03:06,489
It's fluent-ish but drifts off topic, which is typical of a small base model.

41
00:03:06,489 --> 00:03:15,049
One small detail: the comment says twenty more words, but max new tokens counts tokens, and a token can be part of a word.

42
00:03:15,899 --> 00:03:16,673
Why Python?

43
00:03:16,673 --> 00:03:17,517
Two reasons.

44
00:03:17,517 --> 00:03:21,879
It's readable: for word in sentence says exactly what it does.

45
00:03:21,879 --> 00:03:26,452
And the ecosystem: NumPy, PyTorch and Hugging Face already exist.

46
00:03:26,452 --> 00:03:27,719
Isn't Python slow?

47
00:03:27,719 --> 00:03:29,056
Raw Python is, yes.

48
00:03:29,056 --> 00:03:31,588
But it doesn't do the heavy lifting.

49
00:03:31,588 --> 00:03:37,569
When you called pipeline, Python was gluing together highly optimised C and GPU code.

50
00:03:37,569 --> 00:03:40,454
Python conducts, and the orchestra plays.

51
00:03:41,304 --> 00:03:41,937
Part two.

52
00:03:41,937 --> 00:03:43,906
Storing and processing text.

53
00:03:44,756 --> 00:03:46,913
A variable is a labelled container.

54
00:03:46,913 --> 00:03:49,378
Here output and prompt hold two strings.

55
00:03:49,378 --> 00:03:53,568
len gives the number of characters, and type confirms it's a string.

56
00:03:53,568 --> 00:04:00,962
split breaks the text into a list of words, and lower makes everything lowercase first, which is called method chaining.

57
00:04:00,962 --> 00:04:06,507
And output, square bracket, len of prompt, colon, is slicing: everything after the prompt.

58
00:04:06,507 --> 00:04:09,588
When I ran it, the length printed was seventy one.

59
00:04:10,438 --> 00:04:11,460
A small erratum.

60
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:15,357
In the book's text, the comment says this prints sixty eight.

61
00:04:15,357 --> 00:04:20,275
But the real answer, and what the book's own notebook prints, is seventy one.

62
00:04:20,275 --> 00:04:25,449
len counts every character, including the eleven spaces between the twelve words.

63
00:04:25,449 --> 00:04:32,284
It's a trivial slip, but it's a good habit to form right now: never trust a comment over the actual output.

64
00:04:32,284 --> 00:04:32,731
Run it.

65
00:04:33,581 --> 00:04:38,087
Slicing deserves a slide of its own, because we'll use it constantly.

66
00:04:38,087 --> 00:04:41,482
Index zero is the first item, minus one is the last.

67
00:04:41,482 --> 00:04:43,702
Colon three means the first three.

68
00:04:43,702 --> 00:04:46,575
Four colon means from index four to the end.

69
00:04:46,575 --> 00:04:55,520
And two slices we'll see in every training loop: colon minus one, everything except the last, and one colon, everything except the first.

70
00:04:55,520 --> 00:05:00,483
Keep those last two in mind, because they come back in the review questions.

71
00:05:01,333 --> 00:05:10,395
Numbers come in two main kinds: integers like fifty thousand two hundred and fifty seven, and floats like point zero zero zero one.

72
00:05:10,395 --> 00:05:13,024
Underscores make big numbers readable.

73
00:05:13,024 --> 00:05:27,551
And f-strings let you put values straight into text: colon comma adds thousands separators, colon point two e gives scientific notation, and you can even compute inside the braces, like integer division by two.

74
00:05:27,551 --> 00:05:36,267
Fifty thousand two hundred and fifty seven divided by two, rounded down, is twenty five thousand one hundred and twenty eight.

75
00:05:36,267 --> 00:05:39,449
You'll write f-strings in every training loop.

76
00:05:40,299 --> 00:05:41,124
Part three.

77
00:05:41,124 --> 00:05:42,774
Building a vocabulary.

78
00:05:43,624 --> 00:05:45,450
Here's the crucial insight.

79
00:05:45,450 --> 00:05:47,005
GPT-2 never sees words.

80
00:05:47,005 --> 00:05:48,087
It sees numbers.

81
00:05:48,087 --> 00:05:58,975
The book's prompt, the secret to building AI is, becomes four six four, three two hundred, two eight four, two six one five, nine five five two, three one eight.

82
00:05:58,975 --> 00:06:02,897
I checked those with the real tokenizer and they're right.

83
00:06:02,897 --> 00:06:04,453
So we need a tokenizer.

84
00:06:04,453 --> 00:06:11,485
Text goes in, gets lowercased and split, each word is looked up in a vocabulary, and out come token IDs.

85
00:06:12,335 --> 00:06:15,725
First, collect all the words from some training text.

86
00:06:15,725 --> 00:06:18,283
The notebook uses three short sentences.

87
00:06:18,283 --> 00:06:24,486
We loop over them, lowercase and split each one, and use extend to add the words to one big list.

88
00:06:24,486 --> 00:06:26,596
extend adds each item separately.

89
00:06:26,596 --> 00:06:32,095
If you used append, you'd add the whole list as a single item, a classic beginner bug.

90
00:06:32,095 --> 00:06:38,490
Then indexing: the first word is the, the last is examples, and the first three are the, secret, to.

91
00:06:39,340 --> 00:06:43,698
Now the key step: a dictionary mapping each word to a number.

92
00:06:43,698 --> 00:06:46,055
We start with two special tokens.

93
00:06:46,055 --> 00:06:52,341
PAD, ID zero, fills empty space so that sequences in a batch line up to the same length.

94
00:06:52,341 --> 00:06:56,342
UNK, ID one, is the fallback for words we've never seen.

95
00:06:56,342 --> 00:07:05,842
Then for each word, if it isn't in the vocabulary yet, we give it the next free ID, which is just the current size of the dictionary.

96
00:07:05,842 --> 00:07:10,128
Fourteen unique words plus two special tokens gives sixteen.

97
00:07:10,128 --> 00:07:14,129
The IDs follow the order in which words were first seen.

98
00:07:14,979 --> 00:07:15,754
Part four.

99
00:07:15,754 --> 00:07:17,304
Handling edge cases.

100
00:07:18,154 --> 00:07:21,913
What happens with a word that isn't in the vocabulary?

101
00:07:21,913 --> 00:07:26,298
Indexing the dictionary directly raises a KeyError and crashes.

102
00:07:26,298 --> 00:07:32,980
We could check with if and else: if the word is in the vocabulary use its ID, otherwise use UNK.

103
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:36,391
Or more neatly, use dot get with a default value.

104
00:07:36,391 --> 00:07:40,010
That pattern is everywhere in machine learning code.

105
00:07:40,010 --> 00:07:44,117
With our vocabulary, neural maps to one, the unknown token.

106
00:07:44,967 --> 00:07:48,474
Now a whole sentence: the neural network learns.

107
00:07:48,474 --> 00:07:55,124
The loop version goes word by word, gets each ID with a fallback, and appends it to a list.

108
00:07:55,124 --> 00:08:03,965
The list comprehension does exactly the same thing in one line: square brackets, the expression, for each w in the words.

109
00:08:03,965 --> 00:08:06,084
Both give two, one, one, one.

110
00:08:06,084 --> 00:08:07,180
Three unknowns.

111
00:08:07,180 --> 00:08:11,637
Our tiny vocabulary has never seen neural, network or learns.

112
00:08:11,637 --> 00:08:18,433
That's the weakness of word level tokenizers, and subword tokenizers in chapter eight fix it.

113
00:08:19,283 --> 00:08:19,989
Part five.

114
00:08:19,989 --> 00:08:21,544
Functions and classes.

115
00:08:22,394 --> 00:08:24,725
Functions package reusable code.

116
00:08:24,725 --> 00:08:30,041
def, a name, parameters in parentheses, and return sends back the result.

117
00:08:30,041 --> 00:08:33,828
tokenize lowercases, splits, and looks up each word.

118
00:08:33,828 --> 00:08:45,481
detokenize needs the reverse mapping, from ID to word, so it builds one with a dictionary comprehension that flips every pair, then joins the words with spaces.

119
00:08:45,481 --> 00:08:50,360
The secret to AI encodes to two, three, four, six and decodes back.

120
00:08:50,360 --> 00:08:54,584
Notice we lost the capital letters, because we lowercased.

121
00:08:54,584 --> 00:09:04,052
GPT-2's tokenizer gives completely different numbers, from a different vocabulary, but the principle is the same: text to numbers.

122
00:09:04,902 --> 00:09:09,582
Hugging Face organises its tokenizers as classes, so let's do the same.

123
00:09:09,582 --> 00:09:13,866
A class is a blueprint, and an object is one thing built from it.

124
00:09:13,866 --> 00:09:18,217
You can build many objects from one class, each with its own data.

125
00:09:18,217 --> 00:09:24,281
The special method init runs once when the object is created and sets up its starting state.

126
00:09:24,281 --> 00:09:26,522
self means this particular object.

127
00:09:26,522 --> 00:09:29,554
Methods are functions that live on the object.

128
00:09:29,554 --> 00:09:34,299
And defining the special method len lets you call len on your tokenizer.

129
00:09:35,149 --> 00:09:38,131
Here's the full class from the notebook.

130
00:09:38,131 --> 00:09:46,629
init creates both directions of the mapping, word to ID and ID to word, already containing the two special tokens.

131
00:09:46,629 --> 00:09:56,617
fit builds the vocabulary from training texts, assigning each new word the next ID and recording the reverse mapping at the same time.

132
00:09:56,617 --> 00:10:00,791
encode turns text into IDs, using one for unknown words.

133
00:10:00,791 --> 00:10:03,176
decode turns IDs back into text.

134
00:10:03,176 --> 00:10:05,860
And len returns the vocabulary size.

135
00:10:05,860 --> 00:10:09,960
About twenty lines, and it's a real, working tokenizer.

136
00:10:10,809 --> 00:10:13,743
Using it looks just like using Hugging Face.

137
00:10:13,743 --> 00:10:17,211
Create the object, fit it on texts, and call encode.

138
00:10:17,211 --> 00:10:23,412
With GPT-2's tokenizer, you load it with from pretrained and call encode, the same interface.

139
00:10:23,412 --> 00:10:29,613
My run printed a vocabulary of sixteen, the encoding two, three, four, six, and the decoding.

140
00:10:29,613 --> 00:10:37,881
The real tokenizer just has many more features: subword tokenization, more special tokens, saving and loading, and batching.

141
00:10:38,731 --> 00:10:40,752
Our tokenizer is word level.

142
00:10:40,752 --> 00:10:46,091
GPT-2 uses subword byte pair encoding, which we'll build in chapter eight.

143
00:10:46,091 --> 00:10:55,110
Our vocabulary grows with the data, sixteen words here, while GPT-2's is fixed at fifty thousand two hundred and fifty seven.

144
00:10:55,110 --> 00:11:03,696
We map unseen words to UNK, while GPT-2 never needs to, because it can always fall back to smaller pieces or raw bytes.

145
00:11:03,696 --> 00:11:08,891
And GPT-2 keeps case, and treats the leading space as part of the token.

146
00:11:08,891 --> 00:11:13,364
The book says understanding can split into under and standing.

147
00:11:13,364 --> 00:11:23,177
I checked: that's true without a leading space, but with a space in front, as it would appear mid-sentence, GPT-2 keeps it as one token.

148
00:11:23,177 --> 00:11:24,187
Spaces matter.

149
00:11:25,037 --> 00:11:25,787
Part six.

150
00:11:25,787 --> 00:11:26,786
Full circle.

151
00:11:27,636 --> 00:11:29,542
Now we can explain the hook.

152
00:11:29,542 --> 00:11:33,625
When you called pipeline at the start, five things happened.

153
00:11:33,625 --> 00:11:38,320
One: your text went through a tokenizer, like the one you just built.

154
00:11:38,320 --> 00:11:41,655
Two: the IDs became embeddings, from chapter two.

155
00:11:41,655 --> 00:11:48,596
Three: those vectors flowed through twelve transformer layers with attention, chapters three and four.

156
00:11:48,596 --> 00:11:52,611
Four: a token was sampled from the predicted probabilities.

157
00:11:52,611 --> 00:11:54,925
Five: it was decoded back to text.

158
00:11:54,925 --> 00:12:00,097
And then it looped, appending the new token and running again, twenty times.

159
00:12:00,097 --> 00:12:01,934
You've now built stage one.

160
00:12:02,784 --> 00:12:05,356
And here's why those two slices matter.

161
00:12:05,356 --> 00:12:09,247
To train a language model, we take a sequence of token IDs.

162
00:12:09,247 --> 00:12:16,304
The inputs are all tokens except the last, and the targets are all tokens except the first, shifted by one.

163
00:12:16,304 --> 00:12:21,843
At every position, the model sees the tokens so far and has to predict the next one.

164
00:12:21,843 --> 00:12:26,460
The cat sat on the mat gives five training examples from one sentence.

165
00:12:26,460 --> 00:12:30,944
This one line of slicing is at the heart of every LLM training loop.

166
00:12:31,794 --> 00:12:32,605
Part seven.

167
00:12:32,605 --> 00:12:35,037
The exercises, with my solutions.

168
00:12:35,887 --> 00:12:39,062
Here are my solutions to the three exercises.

169
00:12:39,062 --> 00:12:42,730
This is my own code, not from the book's repository.

170
00:12:42,730 --> 00:12:53,736
Exercise one, strip punctuation: a clean helper that lowercases, removes every punctuation character using string dot punctuation and translate, and splits.

171
00:12:53,736 --> 00:12:58,181
Both fit and encode use it, so learning comma becomes learning.

172
00:12:58,181 --> 00:13:03,966
Exercise two, count unknowns: encode each sentence and count how many ones appear.

173
00:13:03,966 --> 00:13:13,279
Exercise three, save and load: dump the word to ID dictionary to JSON, and a class method loads it and rebuilds the reverse mapping.

174
00:13:13,279 --> 00:13:23,509
I ran all three: the punctuation was stripped, my test sentences had three unknown words, and a saved and reloaded tokenizer encoded identically.

175
00:13:23,509 --> 00:13:26,825
The full file is in the repo next to the notes.

176
00:13:27,675 --> 00:13:29,948
One gotcha in exercise three.

177
00:13:29,948 --> 00:13:41,629
JSON keys are always strings, so if you save the ID to word dictionary directly, your integer keys come back as strings, and decoding silently fails.

178
00:13:41,629 --> 00:13:47,586
The fix is to save only word to ID, and rebuild the reverse mapping on load.

179
00:13:47,586 --> 00:13:53,936
Hugging Face does exactly this: GPT-2's vocab dot json maps token strings to IDs.

180
00:13:54,786 --> 00:13:55,490
Part eight.

181
00:13:55,490 --> 00:13:56,258
Wrapping up.

182
00:13:57,108 --> 00:13:59,929
Here's everything from today on one page.

183
00:13:59,929 --> 00:14:01,648
Variables to name values.

184
00:14:01,648 --> 00:14:03,712
Strings, with lower and split.

185
00:14:03,712 --> 00:14:08,459
Lists, with indexing like minus one and slicing like colon minus one.

186
00:14:08,459 --> 00:14:11,279
Dictionaries, with dot get and a default.

187
00:14:11,279 --> 00:14:12,586
Functions with def.

188
00:14:12,586 --> 00:14:16,026
And classes that bundle data and methods together.

189
00:14:16,026 --> 00:14:20,084
That's enough Python to read most of the code in this book.

190
00:14:20,934 --> 00:14:22,314
The key takeaways.

191
00:14:22,314 --> 00:14:31,970
One: you ran a hundred and twenty four million parameter model in about ten lines, because Python orchestrates optimised code.

192
00:14:31,970 --> 00:14:35,648
Two: language models see token IDs, never words.

193
00:14:35,648 --> 00:14:43,312
Three: a vocabulary is a dictionary from words to IDs, with special tokens for padding and unknowns.

194
00:14:43,312 --> 00:14:48,677
Four: encode turns text into IDs, and decode turns IDs back into text.

195
00:14:48,677 --> 00:14:53,811
Five: our SimpleTokenizer has the same interface as Hugging Face's.

196
00:14:53,811 --> 00:15:01,781
Six: the two slices, colon minus one and one colon, give the inputs and targets for next token training.

197
00:15:02,631 --> 00:15:04,950
My notebook run and book check.

198
00:15:04,950 --> 00:15:11,456
The chapter five notebook ran end to end on a CPU, all nine code cells, with no errors.

199
00:15:11,456 --> 00:15:18,112
The GPT-2 token IDs, the vocabulary size of sixteen and the encodings all match the book.

200
00:15:18,112 --> 00:15:23,871
One erratum: the length of the output string is seventy one, not sixty eight.

201
00:15:23,871 --> 00:15:30,826
One clarification: understanding splits into under and standing only without a leading space.

202
00:15:30,826 --> 00:15:38,679
And the notebook pins transformers version four point four six, but it ran fine with a newer version too.

203
00:15:39,529 --> 00:15:42,258
The review questions, with my answers.

204
00:15:42,258 --> 00:15:44,054
One: what does GPT-2 see?

205
00:15:44,054 --> 00:15:50,087
Token IDs, integers from a vocabulary of fifty thousand two hundred and fifty seven.

206
00:15:50,087 --> 00:15:52,242
Two: why use dot get with UNK?

207
00:15:52,242 --> 00:15:59,137
Because looking up an unknown word directly raises a KeyError, while get returns a safe default.

208
00:15:59,137 --> 00:16:08,690
Three: a function is one reusable operation, while a class bundles state, like the vocabulary, together with the methods that use it.

209
00:16:08,690 --> 00:16:17,596
Four: tokens colon minus one is everything but the last token, used as the inputs, while tokens one colon gives the targets.

210
00:16:17,596 --> 00:16:29,016
Five: our tokenizer follows the same pattern as Hugging Face's, create, fit or load, encode and decode, but theirs adds subword BPE, special tokens and saving.

211
00:16:29,866 --> 00:16:31,147
Your practice list.

212
00:16:31,147 --> 00:16:38,696
Open the chapter five notebook in Colab and run every cell, then change the prompt and the number of new tokens.

213
00:16:38,696 --> 00:16:44,020
Set do sample to false and check whether the output becomes the same every run.

214
00:16:44,020 --> 00:16:48,671
Add the three exercise methods and test them with your own sentences.

215
00:16:48,671 --> 00:16:53,524
Compare our tokenizer with GPT-2's on a sentence containing a rare word.

216
00:16:53,524 --> 00:16:56,085
And next up, the chapter six notebook.

217
00:16:56,935 --> 00:16:58,365
That's chapter five.

218
00:16:58,365 --> 00:17:00,940
We can now turn text into token IDs.

219
00:17:00,940 --> 00:17:10,738
But IDs are just the start: next they become vectors, and vectors become matrices, and we need to compute on millions of numbers at once.

220
00:17:10,738 --> 00:17:15,601
That's chapter six, with NumPy and PyTorch, the language of tensors.

221
